    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picts">Picts</a> were the peoples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firth-of-forth">Forth</a>, united from the 6th century under powerful over-kings.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bridei-son-of-beli">Bridei son of Beli</a>’s victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-dun-nechtain">Dun Nechtain</a> in 685 ended Northumbrian rule north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firth-of-forth">Forth</a>.</li><li>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> defeats in the 9th century, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picts">Picts</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaels">Gaels</a> merged into the kingdom of Alba, the core of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Pictish carved symbols, such as the crescent and V-rod and the double disc and Z-rod, appear on many stones across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, yet their meaning has never been deciphered.</p></aside>
    </section>

