    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ammianus-marcellinus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ammianus-marcellinus.jpg" alt="Ammianus Marcellinus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ammianus-marcellinus">Ammianus Marcellinus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ammianus_Marcellinus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Author of the original work of art is long dead, while the uploader of the picture portraying it released it under a ..., CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picts">Picts</a> were the peoples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firth-of-forth">Forth</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-clyde">Clyde</a> in late Roman and early medieval times. Their name first appears in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> panegyric of 297, which mentions <em>Picti</em> alongside the Irish as enemies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. It means ‘painted people’ in Latin, perhaps referring to tattooing or body paint, though it may also have been a Roman nickname that adapted a native word. The Picts were not newcomers but descendants of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> peoples whom earlier Roman writers had called the Caledonii and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maeatae">Maeatae</a>, and of other tribes named by Ptolemy, such as the Vacomagi and Taexali. Their emergence under a new name probably reflects the merging of smaller groups into larger confederacies under pressure from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, much as happened among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> and Alemanni on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ammianus-marcellinus">Ammianus Marcellinus</a> says that in the 360s they were divided into two peoples, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dicalydones">Dicalydones</a> and the Verturiones, the latter probably the ancestors of the later kingdom of Fortriu. Their language, known only from place-names, personal names and a few inscriptions, is now usually regarded as a Brittonic Celtic language related to Welsh, and place-names beginning with <em>Pit-</em>, meaning a share of land, mark their territory. Over the following centuries the many Pictish districts were drawn together under powerful over-kings.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/inverness"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/inverness.jpg" alt="Inverness" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/inverness">Inverness</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Inverness_Castle,_Scotland_-_Diliff.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Diliff, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Pictish society was rural and aristocratic. Most people were farmers who grew barley and oats and raised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, sheep and pigs, living in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> or stone houses, while in the far north and the islands older roundhouse traditions continued. The elite displayed its wealth in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, including massive silver chains and fine penannular brooches; the hoard from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-ninians-isle">St Ninian’s Isle</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shetland">Shetland</a>, buried around 800, shows the skill of Pictish metalworkers. Power centred on fortified sites such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burghead">Burghead</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moray-firth">Moray Firth</a>, the largest known Pictish fort, with its carvings of bulls, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunnottar-castle">Dunnottar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dundurn">Dundurn</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/craig-phadrig">Craig Phadrig</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inverness">Inverness</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picts">Picts</a> are best known for their carved stones. The early symbol stones bear abstract designs such as the crescent and V-rod and the double disc and Z-rod, as well as combs, mirrors and animals; their meaning is unknown, though many scholars think they record names or status. Later cross-slabs, such as those at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aberlemno">Aberlemno</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meigle">Meigle</a>, combine the same symbols with Christian crosses, hunting scenes and warriors. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> spread among the Picts between the 5th and 7th centuries, through missions from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iona">Iona</a> and contacts with Northumbria, and monasteries such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portmahomack">Portmahomack</a> on the Tarbat peninsula, where vellum was prepared for books, became centres of learning and craft.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-dun-nechtain"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-dun-nechtain.jpg" alt="Battle of Dun Nechtain" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-dun-nechtain">Battle of Dun Nechtain</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pictish_Stone_at_Aberlemno_Church_Yard_-_Battle_Scene_Detail.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Greenshed, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Pictish warfare began with raids on Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantius-i">Constantius Chlorus</a> campaigned against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picts">Picts</a> in 305–306, in 367 they took part in the barbarian conspiracy that overran the province, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnus-maximus">Magnus Maximus</a> fought them around 382. In the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> the Picts fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaels">Gaels</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dal-riata">Dál Riata</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-strathclyde">Britons of Alt Clut</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dumbarton-rock">Dumbarton</a>) and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angles">Angles</a> of Northumbria. Their greatest victory came on 20 May 685, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bridei-son-of-beli">Bridei son of Beli</a> defeated and killed the Northumbrian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecgfrith-of-northumbria">Ecgfrith</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-dun-nechtain">Dun Nechtain</a>, often called Nechtansmere, ending Northumbrian domination north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firth-of-forth">Forth</a>. The battle is traditionally thought to be shown on the cross-slab in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aberlemno">Aberlemno</a> churchyard. Pictish kings also had fleets: the Irish annals record that Bridei son of Beli attacked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkney">Orkneys</a> in 682, and that 150 Pictish ships were wrecked in 729. Kings fought one another in recurring civil wars, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oengus-i-of-the-picts">Óengus son of Fergus</a> rose to power through a series of battles in 728–729 before attacking Dál Riata, whose stronghold of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunadd">Dunadd</a> he took in 736. Fortified hilltops, coastal promontory forts with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>-laced stone ramparts and the difficult terrain of the Highlands gave the Picts strong defensive centres, and the kingdom was never conquered by its southern neighbours.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/bede"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bede.jpg" alt="Bede" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:E-codices_bke-0047_001v_medium_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">http://www.e-codices.unifr.ch/de/bke/0047/1v, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Pictish kingdom grew from many regional lordships into an over-kingship that dominated northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. A later medieval tract names seven provinces, Cait (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caithness">Caithness</a>), Ce, Fib (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fife">Fife</a>), Fidach, Fotla (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atholl">Atholl</a>), Circinn and Fortriu, and a list of Pictish kings survives in later copies. Fortriu, once placed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strathearn">Strathearn</a>, is now often located around Moray and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moray-firth">Moray Firth</a>, and from the 7th century its kings were the leading power. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> reports that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picts">Picts</a> chose their kings from the female line when the succession was in doubt, and many Pictish kings had fathers who were not kings, so some historians argue for a matrilineal system, while others doubt it. Royal authority depended on control of fortresses, tribute and the support of regional lords. Kings worked closely with the church. Around 710 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nechtan-mac-der-ilei">Nechtan son of Der-Ilei</a> wrote to Abbot <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceolfrith">Ceolfrith</a> of Wearmouth and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jarrow">Jarrow</a> asking for arguments on the dating of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter-controversy">Easter</a> and for masons to build a stone church, and in 717 he expelled the clergy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iona">Iona</a> who refused the Roman Easter. Constantín son of Fergus, who ruled from 789 to 820 and may have controlled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dal-riata">Dál Riata</a> as well, is commemorated on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dupplin-cross">Dupplin Cross</a> near the royal centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/forteviot">Forteviot</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/hilton-of-cadboll-stone"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hilton-of-cadboll-stone.jpg" alt="Hilton of Cadboll Stone" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/hilton-of-cadboll-stone">Hilton of Cadboll Stone</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hilton_of_Cadboll_nms.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Catfish Jim and the soapdish, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picts">Picts</a> left no literature of their own, and much of what is known of them comes from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adomnan">Adomnán</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/life-of-columba">Life of Columba</a> and the Irish annals, yet their kingdom shaped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>. The Pictish over-kingship created the political core around Fortriu, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fife">Fife</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angus">Angus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perthshire">Perthshire</a> that became the heartland of the kingdom of Alba and so of medieval Scotland, and many Scottish place-names with <em>Pit-</em> and <em>Aber-</em> preserve their language. The victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-dun-nechtain">Dun Nechtain</a> in 685 kept northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> outside the English kingdoms, a division that lasted. Pictish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> linked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iona">Iona</a> and Northumbria, and the cult of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrew-the-apostle">St Andrew</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-andrews">Kinrimund</a>, later St Andrews, which became Scotland’s chief bishopric and gave the country its patron saint, traditionally began under a Pictish king. Pictish art is among the finest of early medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>: the symbol stones and cross-slabs at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aberlemno">Aberlemno</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meigle">Meigle</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hilton-of-cadboll-stone">Hilton of Cadboll</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigg-stone">Nigg</a>, with their mix of abstract symbols, interlace and lively animals, are unique to the region and form one of Scotland’s most recognisable heritage traditions. The symbols themselves remain undeciphered. After the 10th century the Picts disappeared from the record, and later writers often imagined them as a mysterious vanished people, an image that modern archaeology has largely dispelled.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/annals-of-ulster"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/annals-of-ulster.jpg" alt="Annals of Ulster" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/annals-of-ulster">Annals of Ulster</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Annals-of-ulster.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown authorUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Pictish kingdom ended not by conquest but by transformation into the Gaelic kingdom of Alba. Gaelic influence had grown for generations through the church, marriage alliances and the close links between the Pictish and Dál Riatan royal houses; several kings of the early 9th century, among them Constantín son of Fergus and his brother Óengus, may have ruled both peoples. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> attacks broke this order. In 839 the Vikings won a great victory over the men of Fortriu, killing the king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eoganan-mac-oengusa">Eóganán</a> son of Óengus, his brother Bran, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aed-mac-boanta">Áed son of Boanta</a> and, according to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/annals-of-ulster">Annals of Ulster</a>, others almost without number. In the confusion <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kenneth-macalpin">Cináed mac Ailpín</a>, whose origins were probably Gaelic, made himself king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picts">Picts</a> around 842–843; later legends of his treachery against the Pictish nobles are not reliable. The Irish annals still called him and his successors kings of the Picts, but further Viking raids, such as the plundering of Pictland by Olaf and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auisle">Auisle</a> in 866, weakened the old elite, and Gaelic replaced Pictish as the language of power. When Domnall son of Constantín died in 900, the annals called him king of Alba, the first use of the new name, and the Picts disappear from the record soon after.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Kingdom of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picts">Picts</a> united the peoples of northern and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> under powerful over-kings, defeated Northumbria at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-dun-nechtain">Dun Nechtain</a> in 685, produced the distinctive carved symbol stones of early medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, and in the 9th century merged with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaels">Gaels</a> to form the kingdom of Alba, the core of medieval Scotland.</em></p>

